1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,672
The angiogram is over.

2
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:03,785
What happens before you go home?

3
00:00:04,481 --> 00:00:08,313
Recovery starts with checking the place
where the catheter entered, then making

4
00:00:08,382 --> 00:00:11,401
sure you understand the result and your
follow-up plan.

5
00:00:12,005 --> 00:00:15,836
Once the equipment comes out, staff
control bleeding where it entered.

6
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:20,736
At the wrist, they often use a compression
band and ease the pressure as they check

7
00:00:20,805 --> 00:00:25,554
the site. At the groin, the team may apply
pressure or use a closure

8
00:00:25,589 --> 00:00:30,558
device. The way they close the site
depends on the procedure and the patient.

9
00:00:31,162 --> 00:00:35,550
In recovery, staff check your blood
pressure and pulse, look for bleeding, and

10
00:00:35,597 --> 00:00:39,091
assess circulation in the hand or foot on
the side used.

11
00:00:39,602 --> 00:00:44,281
Depending on the access site and how it
was closed, you may be asked to stay lying

12
00:00:44,339 --> 00:00:48,403
down at first. Staff tell you when to sit
up and walk.

13
00:00:49,099 --> 00:00:53,360
A clinician explains what the pictures
showed and what that means for your care.

14
00:00:53,871 --> 00:00:59,258
They may discuss medicines and prevention,
more tests, angioplasty, or a review for

15
00:00:59,328 --> 00:01:04,645
bypass surgery. Sometimes more assessment
is needed because the pictures alone do

16
00:01:04,715 --> 00:01:06,224
not explain the symptoms.

17
00:01:06,828 --> 00:01:10,067
Ask when you will receive any results that
are still pending.

18
00:01:10,764 --> 00:01:14,676
Some people can leave after observation
following a straightforward planned

19
00:01:14,746 --> 00:01:19,715
angiogram. Others need longer monitoring
or a hospital stay,

20
00:01:19,889 --> 00:01:24,394
especially if they have been treated for a
heart attack or had another procedure.

21
00:01:25,207 --> 00:01:29,839
Before you leave, ask for written
instructions, who to contact, and whether

22
00:01:29,897 --> 00:01:31,766
someone should accompany you home.

23
00:01:32,463 --> 00:01:36,979
Your discharge plan should explain what to
do about the dressing and activity,

24
00:01:37,049 --> 00:01:39,975
including lifting, driving and work.

25
00:01:40,927 --> 00:01:44,073
Ask which medicines to take and what has
changed.

26
00:01:44,676 --> 00:01:49,216
A stent can change the medicine plan, so
check with your care team before stopping

27
00:01:49,425 --> 00:01:52,385
or changing medicines prescribed after
one.

28
00:01:52,989 --> 00:01:55,996
A small bruise or mild soreness can occur.

29
00:01:56,275 --> 00:02:01,290
Contact your care team promptly if pain or
swelling increases, if the site

30
00:02:01,325 --> 00:02:05,585
becomes more red or starts draining, or if
you develop a fever.

31
00:02:06,537 --> 00:02:11,483
The team can tell you how to get advice
and what to do if the clinic is closed.

32
00:02:12,435 --> 00:02:17,392
Get emergency help for heavy bleeding,
bleeding that will not stop, or a

33
00:02:17,508 --> 00:02:20,434
rapidly growing swelling at the access
site.

34
00:02:21,247 --> 00:02:26,367
Sudden coldness, paleness or numbness in
the hand or foot on that side

35
00:02:26,541 --> 00:02:28,677
also needs urgent attention.

36
00:02:29,490 --> 00:02:34,483
Severe chest pain or sudden trouble
breathing are emergency symptoms too.

37
00:02:35,179 --> 00:02:39,684
Follow-up is where you can ask what the
findings mean for your symptoms, how the

38
00:02:39,730 --> 00:02:44,340
medicines fit into your plan and whether
another test or treatment is needed.

39
00:02:45,036 --> 00:02:48,960
If the team recommends cardiac
rehabilitation after a heart attack or

40
00:02:49,053 --> 00:02:53,012
angioplasty, the recovery plan can explain
how to join.

41
00:02:53,825 --> 00:02:56,019
Three things are worth having in writing.

42
00:02:56,297 --> 00:02:58,387
What did the angiogram show?

43
00:02:58,631 --> 00:03:01,336
What is your plan for recovery and
medicines?

44
00:03:01,487 --> 00:03:03,902
Who should you contact if you have a
problem?

45
00:03:04,598 --> 00:03:08,731
The answers may be different for someone
who had a test alone and someone who also

46
00:03:08,813 --> 00:03:09,939
had treatment.
